 {:db/id "q-139546" :question/id #uuid "925e17f0-1f91-564c-b150-3d5633a84c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139546" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6df3639cb222756221803446ebc735cf1e7cbcf4a922ed7fce835c0c44fb9aa1.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=5={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=3={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-139546/ma-source-field/1f090b9770e70db74e43d682c2055e4862af9305c728eff79b60b328c69b40f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
