 {:db/id "q-140401" :question/id #uuid "f2b946dc-503a-51e5-98d7-7e814f7abb9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140401" :question/problem "Let $v$ be a non-zero vector from ${R}^{2}$ and let $A$ be the rotation-scaling matrix given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe description of $Av$ in terms of geometric transformations in the plane is given below. What are the missing entries?\n\n*To compute the vector $Av$, we rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\theta =\\text{_____}$ and then scale the result by a factor of $r=\\text{_____}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+18} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =6[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the missing entries are $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $r=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140401/ma-source-field/12f1dc25c686a4a398f6cf80c16ab4815deb5d20ea5a9ed5fd9066ab5d98994a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
