 {:db/id "q-140555" :question/id #uuid "e9992e79-a8b1-5b6c-bb33-ddbd38d4ce02" :question/math-academy-id "q-140555" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+4-4)+({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+({x}_{2}-1)^{2}-4 & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+2, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich defines an empty set. Notice that the equation is equivalent to $\\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(2\\text{i})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})^{2}}=1,$ which has imaginary solutions, so we can interpret this as an imaginary ellipse with semiaxes $2\\text{i}$ and $\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140555/ma-source-field/94b14f222b3efd37f9113983d3036d2b06e8df7851252908238136bddcf069be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
