 {:db/id "q-140997/ma-source-field/2cc566f5dc70eea74b0e6c1de242fc4b4173cee850002c1ebe092c615f758817" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9d3fb41-4434-5c99-a083-41daf1b7f0a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-140997/ma-source-field/2cc566f5dc70eea74b0e6c1de242fc4b4173cee850002c1ebe092c615f758817/1ee02da260e7c5a58934a801efd9f860ca06c299d210b3d50bc11ba3afabbcae" "q-140997/ma-source-field/2cc566f5dc70eea74b0e6c1de242fc4b4173cee850002c1ebe092c615f758817/84c0ff886be6043b09aab34fd9da74ab4993f294e77fc7dafb1ad625a0a8c445" "q-140997/ma-source-field/2cc566f5dc70eea74b0e6c1de242fc4b4173cee850002c1ebe092c615f758817/e4279352324f010d6d88a6c62a9142d4b2a2e3156cd25db6c4b660e805a41dc4" "q-140997/ma-source-field/2cc566f5dc70eea74b0e6c1de242fc4b4173cee850002c1ebe092c615f758817/9d91a1af4a2a39a9b6ed0304866c593f0d21a012baf0d6e5ebc5b827f87133b4" "q-140997/ma-source-field/2cc566f5dc70eea74b0e6c1de242fc4b4173cee850002c1ebe092c615f758817/8e339d40cb94b90d4885536184eaa9364cd571ddec258f09b391fb7c7fea2fa1"] :answer-field/correct "q-140997/ma-source-field/2cc566f5dc70eea74b0e6c1de242fc4b4173cee850002c1ebe092c615f758817/8e339d40cb94b90d4885536184eaa9364cd571ddec258f09b391fb7c7fea2fa1" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-140997" :question/id #uuid "2172cdc2-fb35-5393-84ba-3c3dbc3c7926" :question/math-academy-id "q-140997" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =6,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140997/ma-source-field/2cc566f5dc70eea74b0e6c1de242fc4b4173cee850002c1ebe092c615f758817"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
