 {:db/id "q-141187" :question/id #uuid "66cebf22-0b82-5cf0-a87d-5c8eaa25290f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141187" :question/problem "![](images/46/46edcf2265f41b8fac7931802875e1b9849006834b3509a186ffc7acb510959f.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(-x-5)(x+1)<0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(-x-5)(x+1)<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola  \n\n$$\ny=(-x-5)(x+1)\n$$\n\n  \n\nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e830af773829b1712c6a87955e74411b6ad9921ae1aea58029ca968ab354c0f1.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-5$ or    $x>-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141187/ma-source-field/b53e8aa5a5e73c855db67b23ddf6295e6559df1987acbe3f4271f9574dd14714"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
