 {:db/id "q-141622" :question/id #uuid "a81398bc-73ed-504a-b8b3-ef512650d7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141622" :question/problem "If the expression ${y}^{2}-10y+30$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141622/ma-source-field/ac4eb1430d3eeaffa3e5d6f71fecb89b7d2ec353dde7bf638134a6afd6ed2f81"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
