 {:db/id "q-141738" :question/id #uuid "9955f0fd-f850-5637-ae70-443f1b225d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-141738" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d2f7497d6a11b4ceb994755498ef49bf0ce414601ab812dc55555c769a076ec.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave up?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graph II curves upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-141738/ma-source-field/a560ae60b6edc56c31acad3a619edfc6bdb230476dd31f4400a48b18eea7e67a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
