 {:db/id "q-142315" :question/id #uuid "f59dad02-8b17-598a-a754-71bfff242018" :question/math-academy-id "q-142315" :question/problem "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}+6x-5\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+6x-5\\le 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+6x-5\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-6x+5) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=1$ and $x=5,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/7c/7cfa66b5aa6a3f29e7154e329926a47bbbb0eb45dce3251148986785f09ee383.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le 1$ or $x\\ge 5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-142315/ma-source-field/97ee37bca1a6a8e703d078796ade231c543eee9fd4380cf75fb9d3deea629d8b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
