 {:db/id "q-15133/ma-source-field/1e1037b28b4bfbf7682f4a6184c2f8ff3613aed7833bb6fab3572c72f2e293dc" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d94100e-63ab-58cf-b2c0-0f485eafe140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-15133/ma-source-field/1e1037b28b4bfbf7682f4a6184c2f8ff3613aed7833bb6fab3572c72f2e293dc/914aad4d0420d1799c5c315a7e1271b89a310f23c1b44a95935775721acf1c19" "q-15133/ma-source-field/1e1037b28b4bfbf7682f4a6184c2f8ff3613aed7833bb6fab3572c72f2e293dc/9d8a69b932dffa6507bb45948ccce093ec314f8982fe91eb960e1261fba7848c" "q-15133/ma-source-field/1e1037b28b4bfbf7682f4a6184c2f8ff3613aed7833bb6fab3572c72f2e293dc/beeb23017db28cdbcda59110aa021f42919db26014eff6aa64d7803ce5931ce0" "q-15133/ma-source-field/1e1037b28b4bfbf7682f4a6184c2f8ff3613aed7833bb6fab3572c72f2e293dc/f05de1a85a198fd9b830dfe10da3247ada007055b787b01912ae483be951e0fb" "q-15133/ma-source-field/1e1037b28b4bfbf7682f4a6184c2f8ff3613aed7833bb6fab3572c72f2e293dc/dcf60ad8bd8317968336972ca88989faa92d4a252ba489a3a65847943f90eb41"] :answer-field/correct "q-15133/ma-source-field/1e1037b28b4bfbf7682f4a6184c2f8ff3613aed7833bb6fab3572c72f2e293dc/914aad4d0420d1799c5c315a7e1271b89a310f23c1b44a95935775721acf1c19" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-15133" :question/id #uuid "4a434871-4fea-52cd-80a4-1fd81c159fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15133" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a760f55b6437a39010e4d1a00bdb5fb5390d848dd74347e7736e2ac49cd562b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}+(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+144{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta ))}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-15133/ma-source-field/1e1037b28b4bfbf7682f4a6184c2f8ff3613aed7833bb6fab3572c72f2e293dc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
